# Chapter 2 — The REPL

In Chapter 1 you typed a thing and the console answered. That back-and-forth
has a name, and it is worth slowing down for, because it is the surface you
will spend the most time touching. The prompt is not a lesser part of the
console you graduate away from. It is the console's front counter — the place
you go to ask a quick question, test a hunch, or check whether the thing you
just built actually works.

This chapter is about learning to *trust* the prompt: to read what it prints
back, to understand what it does when you hand it something unfinished, and to
recover cleanly when you fumble.

## Read, Eval, Print, Loop

The four letters are the whole machine, and each one is a real step:

- **Read** — the console takes the text you typed and turns it into a
  structure it can work with. `(+ 1 2)` stops being five characters and
  becomes *a list: the verb `+`, then `1`, then `2`*.
- **Eval** — it works out what that structure means. The verb `+` applied to
  `1` and `2` means `3`.
- **Print** — it shows you the answer.
- **Loop** — it goes back to waiting, ready for your next line.

You can watch all four happen with one tiny expression:

```motoi
(+ 1 2)
```

```
3
```

Read turned the text into a list. Eval reduced the list to a number. Print
showed you the number. Loop put the cursor back. Every session is thousands of
turns around that same wheel.

## The prompt shows you the value of things

At the prompt, you do not have to ask for an answer — the value *is* the
answer. Quote a list and the console hands it right back:

```motoi
(quote (1 2 3))
```

```
(1 2 3)
```

`quote` means *do not evaluate this — take it literally.* So `(quote (1 2 3))`
is the list one-two-three, handed back untouched. This is your first real tool
for telling the console the difference between **a thing to run** and **a thing
to keep**. You will lean on it constantly once data starts flowing.

## A half-finished expression is not an error

Here is the move that separates people who are comfortable at the prompt from
people who are nervous at it. You do not have to fit a whole program on one
line. If you type an opening parenthesis and press return before you have
closed it, the console does **not** panic. It waits. It knows the expression
is not balanced yet, so it holds the line open and lets you keep typing:

```motoi
(define (double x)
  (* x 2))
(display (double 21))
(newline)
```

```
42
```

You typed `(define (double x)` and pressed return with the parentheses still
open. The console understood that you were mid-sentence and waited for the
rest. Only when the parentheses balanced — after `(* x 2))` — did it read the
whole thing and define `double`. **Balance is how the console knows you are
finished.** Count your open parens and your close parens; when they match, the
expression is complete and the console will act.

## Recovering from a fumble

You will mistype a name. Everyone does. Watch what happens when you ask the
console for a word it does not know:

```motoi
(frobnicate 3)
```

```
error: unbound symbol: frobnicate
```

Read this closely, because it is the console being *honest*, not the console
being *angry*. \"Unbound symbol\" means: you used a name, and I have nothing
bound to that name. I do not know `frobnicate`. That is the whole message, and
it is true. The fix is not to fight it — the fix is to define the word, or fix
the typo, and try again. The loop is still running; the prompt is still there;
nothing is broken. An error at the prompt is a fact, not a failure.

When an error surprises you, **Appendix H** is the field guide: it lists the
common ones, what each is really telling you, and the smallest change that
resolves it.

## The prompt is where you check your thinking

Because the loop is instant, the prompt is the cheapest place in the whole
console to test an idea. Not sure how `cond` picks a branch? Ask it directly:

```motoi
(define (sign n)
  (cond ((< n 0) 'neg)
        ((> n 0) 'pos)
        (else    'zero)))
(display (sign -4)) (newline)
(display (sign 0))  (newline)
```

```
neg
zero
```

`cond` walks its branches top to bottom and takes the **first** one whose test
is true. `-4` is less than zero, so `sign` answers `neg`. `0` is neither less
nor greater, so it falls through to `else` and answers `zero`. You did not have
to read a manual to learn that — you asked the console and it showed you. That
is the habit this chapter is really teaching: **when you are unsure, ask the
prompt.** It answers faster than any book, and it never lies about what it did.

## What you just proved

You learned what the four letters of REPL actually do, watched the console hold
a line open for a half-typed expression, read a real error without flinching,
and used the prompt to settle a question about how `cond` behaves. That last
one is the point of the whole chapter: the prompt is a thinking tool. You reach
for it the way a carpenter reaches for a tape measure — constantly, cheaply,
without ceremony.

Next we open a door onto something you can *see*. The screen is where the
console stops being a conversation and starts being a picture.

> **Tip**
>
> If you paste a big expression and the prompt seems to hang, count your
> parentheses. A held-open prompt almost always means one close-paren short.
> Add the missing `)` and the console springs to life.

> **Warning**
>
> `quote` keeps a thing *literal* — it does not evaluate what is inside. So
> `(quote (+ 1 2))` is the three-element list `(+ 1 2)`, **not** `3`. When you
> want the value, drop the quote. When you want the shape, keep it. Mixing
> these up is the single most common early stumble; the prompt will show you
> the difference the instant you try both.

